%[text] %[text:anchor:T_81591A8C] # Feature Extraction for Pump Fault Classification
%[text] To train a machine learning model to detect faults in operational data, you need to extract the best features to train your model. In this example, you will learn to interactively explore, extract, and rank features interactively using the Diagnostic Feature Designer app. Then, you will generate a MATLAB function that can regenerate these features on new data, and use them to train a machine learning model to identify faults in new pump data.
%[text] ## How to use videos in this script
%[text] This exercise contains videos that demo how to use MATLAB Apps. There is **no audio** in these videos. 
%[text] <u>**In MATLAB Online**</u>: Pop them out by clicking on the three dots in the lower righthand corner, then select **Picture-in-Picture.** You can then resize the videos and move them around your screen. The video will always stay on top of whatever you are doing in MATLAB Online.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9C4EDB84] ## Setup
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3D0A6ADA] ### 1. Load Saved Dataset
%[text] Using this [example](https://www.mathworks.com/help/predmaint/ug/multi-class-fault-detection-using-simulated-data.html) (`nPerGroup=30)`, we have generated synthetic flow and pressure data for a triplex pump in normal or faulty states. There are 3 possible fault types: leaking seal, blocked inlet, and worn bearing. The combination of any zero, one, two, or three faults gives 8 possible fault code combinations **from 000 (no fault), 001, ... to 111 (all faults present together).**
%[text] The MAT file `pumpDataComplete.mat` contains preprocessed time series data, uniformly resampled with the initial transients removed. Each row contains timestamped flow rate and pressure data and a corresponding fault code. 
%[text] Load this dataset to get started.
load pumpDataComplete.mat
head(pumpDataInMemory) % View the first 8 rows of the dataset
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6BB51398] ### 2. Partition Data for Training and Testing
%[text] We will set aside 10% of the data for testing the predictive model. This helps prevent overfitting by testing our trained model on new data.
rng("default") % For reproducibility
holdOutTestData = 0.1;

c = cvpartition(pumpDataInMemory.faultCode,'HoldOut',holdOutTestData);
trainData = pumpDataInMemory(training(c),:);
testData = pumpDataInMemory(test(c),:);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3EAF0A88] ## Extract Features Interactively using Diagnostic Feature Designer App
%[text] The [*Diagnostic Feature Designer*](https://www.mathworks.com/help/predmaint/ref/diagnosticfeaturedesigner-app.html) app lets you interactively extract, visualize, and rank a variety of features from a measured or simulated dataset. In this section, you will use the Diagnostic Feature Designer to extract the top relevant features that help differentiate between the different pump fault classes. Then, you will use these features to train a machine learning model.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7065AEFD] ### 3. Open Diagnostic Feature Designer
%[text] Start by opening the Diagnostic Feature Designer app from the **APPS** tab on the toolstrip, or by running the command **`diagnosticFeatureDesigner`** in the command window.
%[text] ![](text:video:47a2)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DF061F96] ### 4. Load Data
%[text] Once opened, start a *New Session* and select the training data from the workspace. Data is loaded as a multi-member ensemble, since multiple datasets are stored in a single variable. 
%[text] You can then review the various signals you are importing. Ensure that the `faultCode` variable is a **Condition Variable**. Condition variables are the data labels that indicate the *condition* of the machine, and the App uses these for grouping and classification. Click **Import** when complete.
%[text] ![](text:video:22b1)
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1853CFC6] ### 5. Explore Data
%[text] Once you have imported the dataset successfully, use the Signal Trace plot to visualize the flow and pressure signals. Select the data you want to visualize and click **Signal Trace**. **Multi-select** `flow` and `pressure` data by holding down **CTRL,** so both datasets are plotted at once.
%[text] Go to the SIGNAL TRACE tab and unmerge the axes so the data shows in separate plots. You are are looking at the first 100 signals of the dataset, for a variety of fault codes, all plotted on top of each other. Of course, it looks like a mess.
%[text] Next, group the signals by the condition variable, `faultCode`. You can then click on the legend to highlight a specific fault code and compare it to the other data. You can use this plot to quickly determine if there are any clear differerences between different fault types. Try zooming in by dragging the edges of the panner below the plot. It's difficult to tell the difference in each fault code by eye! This is why we need to extract features that can better separate our data between the different fault codes.
%[text] ![](text:video:3087)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_597C0849] ### 6. Extract and Rank Features using *Diagnostic Feature Designer*
%[text] The Diagnostic Feature Designer allows you to extract time, frequency, and time-frequency domain features from your data. If you know the features you want to extract, you can do this manually using the dropdown menus in the toolstrip. However, you may not always know ahead of time what features will be the most useful: in this case we can start by using **Auto Features**. Auto Features automatically extracts a default set of features across all domains. You can always go back and add more features.
%[text] ![](text:video:2103)
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_4147] **6.1 Extract Features**
%[text] Hold down **CTRL** to multi-select flow and pressure data, then click on **Auto Features** in the toolstrip. Select Standard features, then click compute. This generates 84 features across more than 200 signals, so it can take a minute or two to compute.
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_473c] **6.2 Rank and View Features**
%[text] Once the features are computed, they are automatically ranked using one-way ANOVA. This suggests that some features are better at separating out the different fault classes. This way, you don't need to use all 84 features to train a machine learning model -- you can use a smaller group of just the top ranked features.
%[text] Click on the FEATURE DESIGNER tab and then select **Feature Table**. Here, you can see all of the features that were generated for each signal in the dataset. Then head back to the **Feature Ranking** plot by clicking on the plot tab.
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_9926] **6.3 Export as a Function**
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_4c2b] Often, you will want to use the same feature extraction computations on new data. To redo all of the work you just did interactively in the app, you can generate a MATLAB function to recompute these features. The app generates code that reproduces both the preliminary data processing, feature extraction, and ranking. 
%[text] From the **Export** dropdown in the FEATURE RANKING tab select *Generate Function for Features.* Choose **`20`** as the Number of Top Features to be used in the generated function. Save the generated function to your current folder.
%[text] ***Note***: You may also export the features directly to the Classification Learner app, allowing you to continue with the interactive workflow towards building a machine learning model to design a classifier to identify different faults. However, we'll use a programmatic approach in the rest of this exercise. If there is time at the end of the exercise, you may can try this in the last section (optional). 
%%
%[text] ## \>\>Checkpoint\<\<
%[text] If you would like to catch up to this point, you may run the following command in the command window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] diagnosticFeatureDesigner("DFD_App_SessionData.mldatx")
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_517BF28B] ## Train a Classification Model to Identify Faults
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4FDF0A38] ### 7. Recompute Features from Generated Function
%[text] We exported a function, `diagnosticFeatures`, which will recompute all of the features we generated in the Diagnostic Feature Designer earlier. This allows us to use the function on new or larger datasets, or on data as it's acquired from new pumps. 
%[text] Compute the features using the generated MATLAB function `diagosticFeatures:`
trainFeatures = diagnosticFeatures(trainData)
%[text] Next, do the same thing to compute features from the test data:
testFeatures = diagnosticFeatures(testData)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5A091427] ### 8. Train Classification Models
%[text] We can now train a variety of classifiers on these features to see which one most accurately predicts the fault codes of the pump. First, train three types of models on the training features:
mdlTree = fitctree(trainFeatures, "faultCode"); 
mdlSVM = fitcecoc(trainFeatures, "faultCode"); 
mdlKNN = fitcknn(trainFeatures, "faultCode");
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D3F1216B] ### 9. Compare Model Performance
%[text] To avoid overfitting our training data, we should test the model performance on data it was not trained on -- our test features.
%[text] Choose a trained model and view the confusion chart and accuracy to see how it performed.
mdl = mdlSVM %[control:dropdown:9251]{"position":[7,13]}
predictTest = predict(mdl, testFeatures);
testGroundTruth = testFeatures.faultCode;
figure;
confusionchart(testGroundTruth, predictTest)
accuracy = 1 - loss(mdl, testFeatures, 'faultCode')
%%
%[text] ### 10. Train Classification Models using *Classification Learner*
%[text] Instead of training models programmatically, you could also interactively train a variety of classification models using the [*Classification Learner*](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/classification-learner-app.html) app. To do so, you can export the features directly from the *Diagnostic Feature Designer* to the *Classification Learner,* or you can load the features directly into the app. 
%[text] ![](text:video:4cba)
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_234d] **10.1 Load Training Data**
%[text] Load **`trainFeatures`** into the Classification Learner app. Choose Resubstitution Validation as a Validation Scheme. Note that this provides no protection against overfitting. Later you will be able to test the trained models against the independent test dataset.
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_040a] **10.2 Train Models**
%[text] At this point, you may train several Machine Learning models from the MODELS menu. Here, we select *All Quick-to-Train*, but you can select any model you like. Click *Train All.* Note that model accuracy is very high for some methods. This is likely due to overfitting due to no validation dataset. This was done because our dataset was very small. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_70b5] **10.3 Test Models**
%[text] Next, you can test the model performance against unseen data. Go to the TEST tab, and load the test data `testFeatures`. If you run the model against the test data, you can see the accuracy drops significantly. Take some time to train and test different models in the app. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_35f3] **10.4 Export Model**
%[text] When you have selected the model that provides the greatest accuracy against the test data, you can **Export** the trained model, or generate a MATLAB function to retrain the model.
%%
